# Build FluentLoop Reading — Complete IELTS Academic Reading Platform

Act as a senior full-stack developer, UI/UX designer, database architect, and AI integration engineer.

Your task is to implement a complete, polished reading-learning module inside my existing FluentLoop application. The application is intended to help English learners improve their reading comprehension, academic vocabulary, and preparation for IELTS Academic Reading.

**Important: inspect the existing project before changing anything.** Understand its framework, routing, styling, authentication, database, component structure, and existing features. Extend the current application instead of rebuilding it from scratch. Preserve its established visual identity and avoid breaking existing functionality.

Do not stop after producing a plan or mockup. Implement the working application, database changes, reading interface, content-management tools, and necessary documentation.

# 1. Main product concept

Create an interactive English reading platform inspired by adaptive reading-learning websites, with a stronger emphasis on academic English and IELTS preparation.

The experience should follow this learning cycle:

1. Select a reading level.
2. Choose a passage.
3. Read an illustrated academic text.
4. Tap or hover over selected difficult words to understand their meaning.
5. Answer comprehension questions.
6. Receive a score and detailed explanations.
7. Save vocabulary and track progress.
8. Gradually practise more challenging passages as proficiency improves.

The platform must be useful without requiring an AI API call every time a user reads a passage.

All published lessons should be stored and reusable. Gemini is an optional tool for generating new content, not a dependency for displaying existing lessons.

# 2. Reading levels and initial content library

Create five reading levels:

- A1 — Beginner
- A2 — Elementary
- B1 — Intermediate
- B2 — Upper-intermediate
- C1 — Advanced

Prepare an initial library of 50 unique reading lessons: 10 for each level.

Use these approximate passage lengths as guidelines:

| Level | Suggested length |
|---|---:|
| A1 | 150–250 words |
| A2 | 200–350 words |
| B1 | 300–450 words |
| B2 | 450–650 words |
| C1 | 600–800 words |

These are initial guidelines, not rigid restrictions. Adapt the passage length to the topic and learning objectives.

A1 and A2 should provide general academic-reading foundations rather than pretending to reproduce the difficulty of the real IELTS Academic test. B1–C1 lessons should progressively introduce academic vocabulary, complex sentence structures, inference, and critical reading.

Organize the content around diverse subjects:

- Science and technology
- Environmental science
- Psychology and human behaviour
- History and archaeology
- Education and learning
- Biology and animal behaviour
- Public health
- Economics and society
- Astronomy and space exploration
- Architecture and urban development

Avoid repetitive passages, identical question sets, and superficial rewrites of the same topic.

Every lesson must have a unique ID, title, level, topic, passage, illustration, vocabulary annotations, questions, answer keys, explanations, estimated reading time, and publication status.

## Content generation requirements

Generate the initial library in manageable batches, such as 10 lessons at a time. Do not put all 50 long passages into a single enormous model response if that risks truncation or malformed content.

If the project has a working Gemini API integration, reuse its existing configuration where appropriate. Otherwise, implement a clearly separated, optional Gemini content-generation service.

Use structured JSON output and a consistent schema. Validate generated content before importing or publishing it.

For each batch, provide a downloadable or importable JSON file if the development environment supports creating files, along with the corresponding illustrations and an import process.

Do not claim that all 50 lessons or images have been generated if only a subset is complete. Track the generation status of every lesson and image.

# 3. One large illustration per reading lesson

Every published passage should have one attractive, wide illustration representing its central topic.

Requirements:

- Prefer a landscape image with a 16:9 aspect ratio.
- Use a consistent editorial illustration style throughout the platform.
- Avoid text embedded inside images, logos, watermarks, and unnecessary decorative clutter.
- Store each image as a reusable asset.
- Associate each image with its lesson using a database field or stable asset path.
- Optimize image sizes and use lazy loading for performance.
- Include appropriate alternative text.
- Provide a fallback image or graceful missing-image state.

Use an image-generation service or model that is actually available in the project. If Gemini image generation is available, use it through a suitable integration. If not, support manually uploading an image.

Do not require image generation when a user manually creates a lesson. A manually uploaded image or a lesson without an image must still be supported.

Generate images in batches where possible. Keep image generation separate from text generation so that a failed image request does not destroy or duplicate an otherwise valid lesson.

